---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/chemistry/foundational/atomic-structure');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const bohrCfg = {
  preset: 'bohr',
  params: [
    { name: 'Z', label: 'atomic number Z', min: 1, max: 20, step: 1, value: 11 },
  ],
  height: 330,
};

const ionisationPoints = [
  [1, 13.6], [2, 24.6], [3, 5.4], [4, 9.3], [5, 8.3], [6, 11.3], [7, 14.5], [8, 13.6],
  [9, 17.4], [10, 21.6], [11, 5.1], [12, 7.6], [13, 6.0], [14, 8.2], [15, 10.5], [16, 10.4],
  [17, 13.0], [18, 15.8], [19, 4.3], [20, 6.1],
];
const ionCfg = {
  points: [{ points: ionisationPoints, label: '1st ionisation energy (eV)', mode: 'both', color: '#d9480f' }],
  x: { min: 0, max: 21, label: 'atomic number Z' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const stepsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'Na has 11 electrons', note: 'Protons and electrons match in a neutral atom — eleven each.' },
    { math: '11 = 2 + 8 + 1', note: 'Shells fill inner-first: K holds 2, L holds 8, the rest spill into M.' },
    { math: 'outermost shell: 1 electron', note: 'A lone electron far from the nucleus is weakly held.' },
    { math: 'Na → Na⁺ + e⁻', note: 'Losing one electron exposes a full, stable shell underneath.' },
    { math: 'Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl', note: 'Chlorine wants exactly one electron. Table salt is that trade.' },
  ],
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>The idea</h2>
          <p>
            Everything you have ever touched — air, water, your own hands, the screen you are
            reading — is assembled from fewer than 120 different kinds of building blocks, and
            each block is an <strong>atom</strong>. An atom is a tiny ledger: <code>Z</code>
            protons in the nucleus, exactly <code>Z</code> electrons balancing them. If someone
            hands you an atom and tells you only the proton count, you already know its element,
            how it bonds, what it smells like when reactive, and whether it will conduct
            electricity — because <em>the outermost electrons decide all of chemistry</em>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The numbers involved are hard to picture. An atom is about 0.1 nanometres across
            (10⁻¹⁰ m); its nucleus is roughly 100,000 times smaller still, at around 10⁻¹⁵ m. If
            an atom were a cathedral, the nucleus would be a housefly on the centre spot —
            carrying, nevertheless, more than 99.9% of the mass. Atoms are almost entirely empty
            space, and the solidity of your chair is not tiny balls touching but the
            electrostatic refusal of electron clouds to overlap.
          </p>
          <p>
            The modern picture took shape in three famous steps. Dalton (1803) argued matter
            consists of indivisible atoms of fixed types. Rutherford's team (1909–1911) fired
            alpha particles at gold foil and found that although most sailed through — evidence
            for empty space — about 1 in 8,000 bounced backward, which shocked Rutherford into
            the nuclear model: a tiny, dense, positive core. Bohr (1913) added that electrons
            occupy fixed <em>shells</em> at specific energies. Build the first twenty elements
            below and watch the shell pattern repeat: 2 electrons in the first shell, then 8,
            then 8 again.
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>a Bohr builder — drag Z through hydrogen to calcium</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="sliderLab" data-config={JSON.stringify(bohrCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>Elements, compounds and mixtures</h2>
          <p>
            An <strong>element</strong> is a substance made of one kind of atom only — carbon,
            oxygen, iron. <strong>Compounds</strong> are elements chemically bonded together in a
            fixed ratio: water is always 2 hydrogens to 1 oxygen, never a bit more or less. The
            bond transforms properties completely: sodium is a metal that fizzes violently in
            water, chlorine is a poisonous green gas, and table salt — their compound — is a
            harmless white crystal you put on chips. A <strong>mixture</strong>, by contrast, is
            just ingredients near each other: air is roughly 78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen, with no
            fixed ratio required and no chemical bond between them. Mixtures keep their
            components' properties and can be separated physically — filter sand from water,
            distil alcohol from a brew, magnetise iron out of sulfur — while separating a
            compound needs a chemical reaction.
          </p>
          <p>
            The classic test of the difference is iron and sulfur. Stir the two powders together:
            a mixture — a magnet still picks out the iron. Heat the mixture until it glows: a
            chemical reaction forms iron sulfide, a new black solid that the magnet ignores and
            that, dropped in acid, releases rotten-egg-smelling hydrogen sulfide (which iron
            alone does not). Fixed ratio, new properties, hard to reverse: compound.
          </p>
          <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a compound does not inherit its ingredients'
          properties. Water is not "hydrogen plus oxygen" added together; both elements are
          flammable-feeling gases, yet the compound douses fires. Bonding makes new substances,
          not blends.</p>

          <h2>Inside the atom: protons, neutrons, electrons</h2>
          <p>
            Three particles do all the work. The <strong>proton</strong> carries charge +1 and
            relative mass 1. The <strong>neutron</strong> carries no charge and also has mass 1.
            The <strong>electron</strong> carries charge −1 but weighs almost nothing — about
            1/1836 of a proton. The number of protons (the <strong>proton number</strong>, Z) is
            the atom's identity card: 1 proton means hydrogen, 6 carbon, 11 sodium, and nothing
            else can share that number. The <strong>mass number</strong> A is protons plus
            neutrons, so a sodium atom of mass number 23 has 23 − 11 = 12 neutrons. In a neutral
            atom, electrons exactly equal protons — the ledger balances.
          </p>

          <h2>Electron shells — and the proof they exist</h2>
          <p>
            Electrons arrange themselves into shells of fixed capacity: 2 in the first, 8 in the
            second, 8 in the third (for the first twenty elements). Sodium, Z = 11, therefore
            fills 2 + 8 and leaves a single lonely electron on the outside. The outermost shell
            is chemistry's control panel: atoms "want" a full outer shell, and everything about
            bonding follows from how far away that goal is.
          </p>
          <p>
            But why believe in shells at all — something no microscope can see? Because energies
            betray them. The <strong>first ionisation energy</strong> is the energy needed to
            pull one electron completely off an atom. If shells were fiction, that energy would
            drift smoothly with atom size. Instead it <em>saw-tooths</em>: it peaks at helium
            (Z = 2), neon (Z = 10) and argon (Z = 18) — precisely where a shell has just
            completed — then plunges at the next element, whose newest electron sits on a fresh,
            farther-out shell, weakly held. Each tooth is a shell; the graph below is the
            fingerprint of the shell structure you just built in fig. 1.
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 2</span><span>first ionisation energy across the first 20 elements — peaks mark full shells</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="plotter" data-config={JSON.stringify(ionCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>Isotopes: same element, different weight</h2>
          <p>
            Atoms of the same element can carry different numbers of neutrons. These are
            <strong>isotopes</strong>: same proton number, different mass number. Hydrogen comes
            in three: ordinary ¹H (no neutron), deuterium ²H (one neutron), radioactive tritium
            ³H (two). Carbon's famous trio is ¹²C, ¹³C and ¹⁴C — and because ¹⁴C is radioactive
            with a half-life of about 5,700 years, the shrinking fraction of it in dead organic
            material lets archaeologists date ancient wood and bone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Isotopes behave almost identically in chemical reactions, because chemistry is played
            by electrons and isotopes have identical electron arrangements; the extra neutrons
            only change the mass, and sometimes the nuclear stability. That near-identity is why
            the periodic table's atomic masses are not whole numbers: they are
            <em>weighted averages</em> over nature's isotope mix. Chlorine is about 75% chlorine-35
            and 25% chlorine-37, so its relative atomic mass is (75 × 35 + 25 × 37) ÷ 100 = 35.5.
            If you ever see a decimal atomic mass, an isotope mixture is hiding behind it.
          </p>

          <h2>Ions and ionic bonding</h2>
          <p>
            Atoms reach a full outer shell by transferring electrons, and the result is an
            <strong>ion</strong>: lose electrons, become positive (a cation); gain them, become
            negative (an anion). The full story of table salt, end to end:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 3</span><span>from electron count to table salt</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="equationStep" data-config={JSON.stringify(stepsCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Once formed, Na⁺ and Cl⁻ attract each other with straightforward electrostatics, and
            they assemble not into tidy pairs but into a giant <strong>ionic lattice</strong> — a
            three-dimensional checkerboard of alternating charges, where every cation is
            surrounded by anions and vice versa. That structure explains salt's properties:
            melting requires breaking strong attractions throughout the lattice, so the melting
            point is high (801 °C); solid salt does not conduct because the ions are locked in
            place, but <em>molten</em> or dissolved salt conducts beautifully because the charged
            ions are finally free to move. And the brittleness — hit a salt crystal and it
            shatters, not dents — happens because one shove slides a layer so like charges line
            up and the lattice tears itself apart. Formulas follow from charge balance:
            magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine Cl⁻, so magnesium chloride must be MgCl₂ — two
            single-negative ions per double-positive one.
          </p>
          <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the sodium ion is not "sodium that wants one more
          electron". Na⁺ has the exact electron arrangement of neon (2, 8) — a full outer shell.
          Ions are stable, satisfied configurations; it is the <em>neutral</em> sodium atom that
          was eager to react.</p>

          <h2>Covalent bonds: sharing instead of stealing</h2>
          <p>
            When two non-metals meet, neither can afford to give electrons away (both would need
            to <em>gain</em> to complete a shell), so they share. Two hydrogen atoms each
            contribute their lone electron into a shared pair that spends its time between both
            nuclei, gluing them together: the molecule H₂. Methane shares four: one carbon with
            four hydrogens, CH₄. Water shares two per oxygen. A shared pair acting as
            electrostatic glue between two particular nuclei is a <strong>covalent bond</strong>,
            and it is fiercely strong — splitting water's O–H bonds takes real energy.
          </p>
          <p>
            Yet simple molecular substances — water, carbon dioxide, paraffin — melt and boil at
            low temperatures. The resolution: within each molecule the bonds are strong, but
            <em>between</em> molecules there are only weak attractions. Boiling water breaks no
            O–H bonds at all; it merely persuades whole H₂O molecules to let go of each other.
            Molecules also carry no charge, so simple molecular substances do not conduct
            electricity in any state. Strong inside, weak outside, never conducting — that
            triple signature identifies a simple molecular substance on sight.
          </p>

          <h2>Giant covalent structures and metals</h2>
          <p>
            A few substances take the covalent bond to the extreme: networks of covalent bonds
            with <em>no</em> separate molecules at all. <strong>Diamond</strong> is carbon where
            every atom is bonded to four neighbours in a rigid tetrahedral web — the hardest
            natural material, with a melting point above 3,500 °C, and an electrical insulator
            because every electron is locked in a bond. <strong>Graphite</strong> is also pure
            carbon, but each atom bonds to only three, forming flat sheets of hexagons with one
            spare electron per atom left delocalised — so graphite conducts electricity (rare
            for a non-metal), the sheets slide over each other so it works as pencil "lead" and
            lubricant, and its melting point is just as extreme. Silicon dioxide (sand) is a
            third giant network. Same element, different network, opposite properties: structure
            is destiny.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Metals</strong> play a third bonding strategy. Their few outer electrons
            abandon individual atoms entirely and form a mobile "sea" of delocalised electrons
            flowing around a lattice of positive ions. The mobile sea explains metals' signature
            behaviour at a stroke: it carries charge (conduction), it carries heat energy
            (thermal conduction), and — because the ions sit in this glue rather than in
            directional bonds — the layers can slide past each other, so metals bend and
            hammer rather than shatter. Alloying deliberately ruins the regularity: adding
            carbon or other atoms of awkward size to iron makes steel, whose distorted lattice
            resists sliding, trading a little of copper's workability for far more strength.
          </p>

          <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            The 2-8-8 shell story is a beautifully useful lie. Quantum mechanics says electrons
            live in <em>orbitals</em>, and shell capacities actually run 2, 8, 18, 32 (the
            pattern 2n²). For the first twenty elements the simple counting happens to work,
            but potassium and calcium really fill the 4s orbital before the 3d — which is why
            the periodic table's fourth row suddenly sprouts a block of ten transition metals,
            all fussing with d-electrons. Look back at fig. 2 for subtler evidence: the small
            dip at beryllium→boron happens because boron's new electron sits in a higher-energy
            2p orbital, and the dip at nitrogen→oxygen because oxygen's paired 2p electrons
            repel each other. The graph remembers everything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ionisation energies also hold the periodic table's deepest secret in advance:
            helium's 24.6 eV towers over lithium's 5.4 eV, not because helium's nucleus pulls
            harder on <em>any</em> electron but because its two electrons are in the innermost
            shell, closer to the charge. In quantum language, electrons in inner shells
            <em>shield</em> outer electrons from the nucleus, and the balance between nuclear
            charge and shielding, marching across each period, is what makes elements get less
            metallic (easier to oxidise) toward the right of every row — the trend the
            periodic-table lesson builds on. One number to carry away: pull a second electron
            off sodium and the cost jumps from about 5 eV to 46 eV, because now you are breaking
            into a full shell. Sodium will lose one electron; it will essentially never lose two.
            Chemistry's habits are all in the shells.
          </p>

          <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Chemistry 0625 syllabus (2026–2028), sections 2.1–2.7; Rutherford, "The Scattering of α and β Particles by Matter" (1911); OpenStax, <em>Chemistry 2e</em>, chapters 2 and 6; NIST Atomic Spectra Database (ionisation energies).</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
